            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Computes the natural log of the absolute value of the gamma function for every element of a tensor.\n\nIt gives logs of factorials - gammaln(n + 1) is log(n!) - so it is how you compute counts of combinations and probabilities that involve huge factorials without overflowing.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nFor positive x: high near 0, falling to 0 at x = 1, a shallow dip to -0.121 at 1.46, back to 0 at x = 2, then rising steadily and a little faster than a straight line. gammaln(11) = 15.104 = log(10!). The gamma function itself would overflow past x = 35; its log is still a modest number there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.gammaln\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n0 and negative whole numbers give infinity. Between the negative whole numbers it gives the log of the absolute value, so the sign of gamma is lost there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0.1 to 10 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is log gamma of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0.1 to 10, drawn from -1 (bottom) to 13 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (keep start above 0).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.digamma is its slope. t.special.multigammaln is the version for several dimensions (with p = 1 it equals this).",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 720,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Computes the natural log of the absolute value of the gamma function for every element of a tensor.\n\nIt gives logs of factorials - gammaln(n + 1) is log(n!) - so it is how you compute counts of combinations and probabilities that involve huge factorials without overflowing.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nFor positive x: high near 0, falling to 0 at x = 1, a shallow dip to -0.121 at 1.46, back to 0 at x = 2, then rising steadily and a little faster than a straight line. gammaln(11) = 15.104 = log(10!). The gamma function itself would overflow past x = 35; its log is still a modest number there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.gammaln\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n0 and negative whole numbers give infinity. Between the negative whole numbers it gives the log of the absolute value, so the sign of gamma is lost there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0.1 to 10 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is log gamma of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0.1 to 10, drawn from -1 (bottom) to 13 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (keep start above 0).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.digamma is its slope. t.special.multigammaln is the version for several dimensions (with p = 1 it equals this)."
        },
